A||||9||Coconut||Black Paint Blues||||5||
Z||000000||Coconut||05-08-2000||09:56 PM||||When I bought my GT a few years back the paint seemed to be ok. Lately, I've noticed that the nose (especially the area above the parking lights and below the headlights on both sides) have developed fine "cracks" almost like a spider web type effect. What the heck would cause something like that and what would you guys suggest to help remedy the situation until I can afford a GOOD paint job? I've considered a Bra,but I'm not really into that look. Any input?  TIA<P>Coconut ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||05-08-2000||10:43 PM||||2 things to try are:<BR>1) Buff area with water and rubbing compound using light pressure. Then put on 2 coats of Meguire's Deep Crystal Polish (you can do the whole nose) Where you used the rubbing compound buff aggresively. Then wax with 2 coats. This will make them very faint. You will have to use the polish and wax about every 2 months.<P>2) There is a "colored" wax that is suppose to "hide" small imperfections. <P>I personally have never used the colored wax but I hear it works. The reason it happens is 12-14 years tends to dry out paint. Keeping a good coat of wax and using the Meguire's deep crystal polish will keep the paint protected. My car gets polished every spring and waxed at least 1 per month. In the fall I give it a double coat to help protect it during the winter. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||05-08-2000||10:52 PM||||Those fine cracks are just that, cracks.  Usually they go all the way through or at least very deep.  They are caused by stresses in the fibre-glass.  In order to fix them properly they must be ground right down and re-fibred.  Get it done by a good body shop that has experience with fibre-glass.  I suggest a Corvette shop.  If you just paint over top of them they will come back.  Might as well get it done properly or else don't waste your money.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-08-2000||11:09 PM||||The cracks are in the paint not the actual nose! The nose of the Fiero is NOT fiberglass. It is a flexable urethane composite. The flexability of 12-16 year old paint is decreased and thats what causes the cracks.||||reg||4
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||05-08-2000||11:11 PM||||The Front fascia on the fiero is not fiber-glass. Oreif's recommendation would be the best until you get it repainted. I have used a color back wax, after polishing and it did remove/cover/hide the small imperfections?<P>And what's with this green color that you my be putting on????  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||05-08-2000||11:13 PM||||Thanks Oreif, By 2min||||reg||14
Z||000006||Coconut||05-09-2000||12:33 AM||||Dunno- My brother had a Cougar with that green I was talking about and first time I saw it I asked him the same question- WHAT IS UP WITH THAT PUKEY GREEN?!? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Funny thing is, the darn thing starts to grow on ya. After a few weeks it actually started to look pretty good .  LOL <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jay||05-09-2000||03:15 PM||||Sorry, you're right, the nose is not fibre-glass.  I'm thinking about what happens to kit-cars! It happened to my 72 Dino replica which was fibre-glass.  My apologies!||||reg||1
Z||000008||lennyb4||05-09-2000||03:33 PM||||You are right about one thing jay. The cracks in the original paint MUST be removed all the way, otherwise they will eventually come back through the new paint job. Also when you do get it painted make sure the shop uses a good quality flex agent in thier mix.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Uaana||05-10-2000||03:08 AM||||You guys are right about the cracks, been working on mine doing the prep for a maaco paint job, and those cracks in the nose go down to the primer level. Spent the better part of a saturday hand sanding the entire nose and still have the lower edge to do but after I did a coat of primer and sanded with 1000 and wet sanded with 1500 its smoother than the rest of the paint on my car.||||reg||1
